This project aimed to build a system that detects meteors by listening for their 
unique "ping" in the radio spectrum. Instead of using complex and expensive radar, we 
created a passive listener. The idea is simple: a distant radio station (our "beacon") is 
always transmitting. When a meteor burns up in the atmosphere, it leaves a 
temporary trail of ionized gas. This trail can act like a mirror, briefly reflecting the 
beacon's signal toward our antenna. Even though the meteor itself might be invisible, 
we can catch its reflection. 
Our goal was to design the antenna, build the data processing pipeline, and create an 
autonomous system to spot these fleeting signals. 
